  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThis is a very complex and uncertain forecast with how today's \nconvection will unfold. As of 06Z, a cluster of thunderstorms is\nmaintaining itself as it tracks southeast across southeastern \nNebraska. However, it will move into a poorer environment as it \ntracks southeast, with weaker instability and shear. As a \nresult, anticipate this area to diminish/ dissipate with time \nand southeastward extent. This area seems to be on the surface \nfront with reinforcement from the cold pool of the cluster of \nstorms. As that boundary moves south through the area later this\nmorning into the afternoon, it's possible that additional \nstorms develop in its vicinity. However, looking through \nforecast soundings for eastern Kansas and western Missouri, \nthere is considerable convective inhibition to overcome. With \nminimal larger-scale forcing and weak forcing on the boundary, \nit's possible little or no activity is able to develop. Another \nscenario is that, despite the surface based inhibition, there is\nample elevated instability that may provide enough for storms \nto develop. Additionally, the NAM remains very robust in its \ninstability forecast and that may allow for storms develop \nwithin the afternoon warmth along the boundary. With so much \nuncertainty in the forecast, relied heavily on ensemble guidance\nto derive PoPs for today. That generally depicts slight chance \nto low chance PoPs (15- 30%) for our western to southwestern \nzones this morning. Then just slight chances (15-20%) for the \nafternoon and evening hours before increasing chances during the\nlate evening and overnight hours. Overnight, this method \ndepicts an area of 30-40% chances across eastern Kansas and \nwestern Missouri. With regards to the potential for severe \nstorms, this is conditional on if we actually see storms, \nespecially during the afternoon/evening when instability is the \ngreatest. If storms are able to develop, deep-layer shear of \n30-40 kts would support some organized storms with a damaging \nwind and large hail risk.\n\nNext week, ensemble guidance shows upper-level ridging, with well \nabove normal 500mb heights building over the southeastern US, \nspreading into the local forecast area. Northern Missouri is likely \nto be on the northern edges of this upper ridging. Additionally, it \nlooks like a quasi-stationary boundary sets up across northern \nMissouri/ southern Iowa, unable to move further south due to the \nstrong ridging. This setup leads to multiple rounds of storms across \nnorthern Missouri. Cumulatively, from Sunday through the end of next \nweek, widespread 2-5\" rainfall looks possible from the repeated \nrounds of showers and storms. South of this, temperatures will have \nthe potential to be well above normal, with highs in the low to mid \n90s possible. With our normal temperatures now near 80 degrees, \nhighs will potentially be 10-15 degrees above normal from roughly \nthe Missouri River southward. These may be lower, if cloud cover can \nintrude further south and inhibit insolation. Additionally, stronger \nridging could shift the heavier rain further north leading to warmer \ntemperatures than currently forecast.",
